# Prettier's ignore scope for the whole workspace. Every `check` script points here with
# --ignore-path, so build output and generated code are excluded once, at the root, instead of
# once per app. Patterns are gitignore syntax; the ones without a slash match at any depth, so
# they hold whichever directory prettier runs from.

# Dependencies and package-manager state
node_modules/
pnpm-lock.yaml

# Build output — `build` rewrites it, so any formatting applied here is undone by the next run
out/
dist/
.output/
.vite/
.tanstack/
.turbo/
coverage/

# Generated code — owned by its generator, which rewrites it wholesale
routeTree.gen.ts
plugins/*/scripts/lib/

# Prose that prettier does not own.
#
# The only `check` script in the workspace is apps/maestro's, so these directories were never
# actually checked — but nothing said so, and running prettier from the root reported ~110
# failures that read as rot rather than as scope. They are documentation, task files and SKILL.md
# bodies: hand-formatted, read as much in a GitHub blob and a Claude context window as in an
# editor, and reflowing them would rewrite line breaks that were chosen. Stated here so the answer
# is a decision on the record instead of a surprise for whoever next runs prettier from the root.
#
# EVERY PATTERN IS ROOT-ANCHORED, and that is load-bearing rather than tidy. A bare `skills/`
# matches at any depth (the note at the top of this file), so it would also swallow
# apps/maestro/.claude/skills/ — six architecture docs that ARE in the enforced set today. The
# leading slash is what keeps this widening out of the one app prettier is actually policing.
/docs/
/skills/
/rules/
/agents/
/incubator/
/plugins/
/.claude/

# NOT ignored, and deliberately: `packages/` and the root's own README/CHANGELOG/TODO. Those are
# maintained files, not prose prettier has no business in. Twenty-three of them fail today — 20
# TypeScript/TSX under packages/ui and packages/claude-fs, plus three CLAUDE.md/README.md — and
# that is a real gap rather than a scoping question: those packages simply have no `check` script
# to fail. Closing it is a diff to those files, not a line here.
